// RecordingStore decorates a Store to record ref mutations two ways:
//
//   - NATS RefEvents for EVERY repo, via events (the universal notification
//     stream). Only the RSL ref itself is excluded.
//   - gittuf RSL ReferenceEntries for repos the gate enables, via rslStore.
//
// Restore mode disables both. Recursion is impossible by construction: the RSL
// writer's own update to refs/gittuf/reference-state-log reaches the raw store
// through a path that does NOT include this decorator (makeOpenRepoBare).
type RecordingStore struct {
	storeForwarder

	// events is the NATS notification sink. It covers every repo, minus the
	// whole refs/gittuf/** namespace: server-managed trust material produces no
	// ref event, because those fan out to mirror sync and CI webhooks and
	// neither should react to a trust publication. May be a no-op.
	events   refevents.Sink
	rslStore rslstore.Store // gated gittuf RSL writer. Nil disables RSL.
	gate     RSLGate
	// refPolicy is the single source of truth for recording scope: a ref is
	// signed into the RSL only if it is client-visible and outside the carve-outs
	// (refpolicy.Policy.ShouldRecord), and inside the repo's coverage mode
	// (refpolicy.CoveredByMode). Nil falls back to the built-in default resolver
	// so existing callers need no change.
	refPolicy refpolicy.Resolver
	// gittufAncestry enforces the fast-forward-only invariant on refs/gittuf/**.
	// Nil disables the guard (see GittufAncestry); production wires it.
	gittufAncestry GittufAncestry
	// trustLifecycle resolves a repo's trust lifecycle for the rooted-policy pin.
	// Nil disables the pin (test paths without a repostore); production wires the
	// same caching lookup the RSL writer uses.
	trustLifecycle rslstore.LifecycleFunc
	// rslCoverage resolves a repo's RSL coverage mode, which narrows WHICH
	// recordable refs get an entry (ADR 20260905 section 2.3). Nil means the
	// pre-coverage behaviour, full: every recordable ref. That is the right
	// default for an unwired test path, and production always wires it.
// filtering (ensureLifecycleAdmitsWrite), so exempting it here would make a
// seedless node the more permissive of the two.
//
// An admitted write gets the coverage label to report. A repo whose lifecycle
// records nothing is trust_disabled, the label the seedless gate gives the
// same repo. Every other admission is node_disabled, because this node cannot
// record and the repo's lifecycle is not the reason.
