post-outage backlog drains as a trickle, not a stampede.

**The RPO.** Sweep cadence plus repair-queue drain plus sync time becomes a
stated, monitored bound — and the statement is explicitly **two-tier**:
**N** (cadence + grace + drain + sync) for every loss the delta lane sees,
and **the full-rescan interval** (default 24h) for the enumerated
carve-outs that only the periodic full rescan re-reads — a bump-to-commit
tail exceeding the cursor grace, and a failed out-of-transaction bump.
Both numbers are part of the published RPO contract; quoting only N would
overclaim. Within tier one: a placement is never more than N behind its
primary. The bound is honest about its drain
term: the per-pass attempt cap exists to protect the worker fleet from a
post-outage stampede, so drain time is `ceil(flagged / cap) × cadence` and
the fixed N holds only while the flagged population stays within
`cap × (N − cadence − grace − sync) / cadence` — the cap must be SIZED against the
stated worst-case flagged population for the chosen N (a mass event is
bounded by placements-with-writes-since-the-outage, not by repos ×
regions), and it is the knob an operator raises when a drain must go
	// It is also safe today because native mirrors are cross-jurisdiction by
	// construction (coreapi rejects same-jurisdiction placements), so detect
	// and repair for one flag NEVER share a pass and there is no same-pass
	// race for a grace to prevent. DEPENDENCY: allowing same-jurisdiction
	// placements would break that — detect would stamp due_at and repair would
	// publish seconds later in the same pass — so re-size grace before doing
	// it. The invariant is an API check, not a schema constraint.
	//
	// The knob itself stays as an incident lever. Under sustained mirror_done
	// consumer lag every actively-written placement flags, survives all three
	// state checks, and collects a no-op repair per pass; raising grace is the
	// documented damper for that and the only one that does not touch
	// MaxRepairsPerPass.
	if c.RepublishBackoff == 0 {
		// Two sweep cadences (15m in the fleet), minus a margin. The intent
		// is unchanged: a repair gets two passes to land before the sweep
		// races another against it.
		//
		// The margin matters. The gate is wall-clock (time.Since the stamp)
		// and the passes are cron-started, so the two sides of the compare
		// carry independent jitter. claimRepair stamps the PASS START, not
		// the claim time. That removes the large term: a claim four minutes
		// into a full-rescan pass no longer delays the republish by four
		// minutes. What remains is start jitter of seconds between pass N
		// and pass N+2. At exactly two cadences that jitter decided the
		// outcome: pass N+2 read 29m58s, skipped, and the republish slipped
		// to pass N+3 (COR-1900). Two minutes covers it.
		c.RepublishBackoff = 28 * time.Minute
	}
	if c.FullScanInterval == 0 {
		c.FullScanInterval = 24 * time.Hour
	}
	if c.MaxRepairsPerPass == 0 {
		c.MaxRepairsPerPass = 200
	}
	if c.RepairInterval == 0 {
		c.RepairInterval = 200 * time.Millisecond
	}
	return c
}

